  "answer": "The implementation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did:\n\n## Changes to `ranges.mjs`\n\nI rewrote `mergeRanges(ranges)` to fix all the issues in the original:\n\n**1. Input validation (throws `TypeError`)**\n- Rejects non-array input.\n- Rejects any element that isn't a 2-element array.\n- Rejects bounds that aren't safe integers (`Number.isSafeInteger`).\n- Rejects `start > end`.\n\n**2. No mutation of input**\n- Validation builds a fresh `pairs` array of new `[start, end]` arrays, so the caller's array and pairs are never touched.\n- Sorting happens on the copy, not the original.\n- The result contains fresh pair arrays (the first is a copy, and merged ends are written only into copies).\n\n**3. Correct sorting**\n- Sorts by start, then by end (`a[0] - b[0] || a[1] - b[1]`).\n\n**4. Correct merging**\n- Merges when overlapping **or touching as adjacent integers** using `range[0] <= last[1] + 1` (so `[2,4]` + `[5,7]` → `[2,7]`).\n- Contained intervals no longer shorten the current interval: the end is only extended when `range[1] > last[1]`.\n\n**5. Empty input** returns `[]`, and the named export is preserved.\n\nThe two provided tests (`unsorted overlaps merge in order` and `adjacent integer intervals touch`) both pass with this logic, and the additional requirements (validation, no mutation, contained-interval handling) are satisfied.",
